.he "telnetd (8)                   11/03/26                    telnetd (8)"
.fo "                               -#-"
.in 5
.rm 65
.bd
NAME
.in +5
.br
.bd
telnetd
- the telnet server
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
SYNTAX
.in +5
.br
.bd 2
/bin/telnetd [ port ]
.br
/bin/telnetd -i <peer> <pid>
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
DESCRIPTION
.in +5
.br
Telnetd serves one login per connection.  It puts /bin/login on a pty
slave and shuttles bytes between the master and the connection, so the
account and password are asked on the pty and the shell that follows
has its terminal, its line discipline and its job control.
.sp
The telnet protocol is filtered at both ends: IAC sequences from the
client are taken out of the byte stream (IAC IAC is a literal 255), and
a literal 255 from the pty is doubled on the way out.  Option
negotiation is refused - WILL is answered DONT and DO is answered
WONT - which leaves the terminal in plain NVT mode.  Subnegotiations
are skipped.
.sp
The port defaults to 23; the command-line port exists for the
simulator, which binds a high host port for the guest.  Under inetd
the connection is already on fd 0 and the arguments are the caller's
dotted quad and inetd's pid.
.sp
The daemon serves one request per process when run by inetd, and forks
a child per connection when it owns the listener.
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
FILES
.in +5
.br
/dev/ptyp0, /dev/ttyp0	the first pty pair
.br
/dev/ptyp1, /dev/ttyp1	the second
.br
/bin/login	the login program
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
SEE ALSO
.in +5
.br
telnet (1), inetd (8), login (1), getty (8)
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
BUGS
.in +5
.br
There is no terminal-type negotiation, so the remote termcap entry is
the caller's business.  Every option is refused rather than
negotiated.
